# grid: 43 images x effort {7,8,9} = 129 cells, lossless
#
# VERDICT (scripts/lz77_ab_compare.py):
#   129/129 cells BYTE-IDENTICAL (sha256), every class x effort mean +0.000%.
#   The libjxl 20% benefit floor inside apply_lz77 (bit_decrease >
#   total_symbols*0.2+16, costs from SymbolCostEstimator over the learned
#   multi-context ANS) declines on every natural-content section: the
#   tree-learned predictors turn value-runs into near-free zero residuals,
#   so RLE/greedy matches save almost nothing per symbol. This is the same
#   gate libjxl uses — declining is parity behaviour, not a wiring failure.
#
# Wiring proof (the fix IS live):
#   - tests/it/lossless_multigroup_lz77.rs: e8/e9 fire on tile-repeated
#     LCG noise (with < without), all efforts pixel-exact via jxl-rs +
#     jxl-oxide; identity on non-repetitive noise (bytes equal on/off).
#   - CLI cell: 512^2 64-px-tiled noise at e8 = 584,559 B (pre-fix) ->
#     71,089 B (post-fix), -87.8%.
#   - terminal.png e8 lossless context: ours 38,511 B vs cjxl 0.12.0
#     80,815 B — we are far ahead of cjxl on screenshot-class regardless.
#
# Conclusion: capability unlock with zero RD movement on natural content;
# the win surface is genuinely repetitive content (sprite sheets, tiled
# textures, generated imagery). Decode-verify sweep: 43 cells e7 + the
# synthetic firing cells e7/8/9, djxl + jxl-rs (jxl_cli) + jxl-oxide, all
